  "a4b-s05": {
    id: "a4b-s05", name: "Two views of Jacksonian democracy", intro: "Use the passages below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [
      { title: "Source 1", blocks: [
        { p: "The politics of the Jackson years are best understood as a revolt of ordinary Americans against privilege. Farmers, mechanics, and small shopkeepers believed that bankers, speculators, and holders of special charters used government to enrich themselves. Jackson's supporters saw the national bank as the clearest example of such a monopoly. When states dropped property tests for voting and new party organizations turned out record numbers of voters, these people gained a voice in national affairs. Jackson's battles with the bank and with Congress expressed their demand that government serve the many rather than the few." },
        { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
      ] },
      { title: "Source 2", blocks: [
        { p: "Calling the Jackson years an age of the common man hides who was left out. The same decades that opened the ballot to poor white men closed it to others: several states that dropped property tests also barred free Black men from voting, and women gained no political rights. The Democratic coalition depended on Southern slaveholders and on Western settlers hungry for land, and its leaders carried out the forced removal of Native nations. Democracy in this period expanded mainly for white men, and often at the direct expense of other groups." },
        { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
      ] },
    ],
  },
  "a4b-s06": {
    id: "a4b-s06", name: "The Senate censures Jackson, 1834", intro: "Use the excerpts below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [
      { title: "Source 1", blocks: [
        { p: "Resolved, That the President, in the late Executive proceedings in relation to the public revenue, has assumed upon himself authority and power not conferred by the Constitution and laws, but in derogation of both." },
        { cite: "Resolution of the United States Senate, March 28, 1834, as quoted in Andrew Jackson, Message to the Senate Protesting Censure Resolution, April 15, 1834.", url: "https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/message-the-senate-protesting-censure-resolution", kind: "primary" },
      ] },
      { title: "Source 2", blocks: [
        { p: "Tested by these principles, the resolution of the Senate is wholly unauthorized by the Constitution, and in derogation of its entire spirit. ... The President is the direct representative of the American People, but the Secretaries are not." },
        { cite: "Andrew Jackson, Message to the Senate Protesting Censure Resolution, April 15, 1834.", url: "https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/message-the-senate-protesting-censure-resolution", kind: "primary" },
      ] },
    ],
  },
  "a4b-s09": {
    id: "a4b-s09", name: "Landscape and a national culture", intro: "Use the passage below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "In the decades after the War of 1812, American writers and painters set out to prove that the young nation could produce a culture equal to Europe's. Lacking ancient castles and ruins, they turned to what they believed Europe could not match: the American wilderness. Novelists told stories of frontier scouts and Native warriors in the forests of New York, and painters filled large canvases with the gorges, waterfalls, and mountains of the Catskills and the Hudson Valley. Nature became a source of national pride and of religious feeling. Yet many of these works also carry a sense of loss, as if the artists knew the wild places they celebrated were already disappearing." },
      { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
    ] }],
  },
  "a4b-s10": {
    id: "a4b-s10", name: "A Methodist preacher remembers the revivals", intro: "Use the excerpt below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "Ten, twenty, and sometimes thirty ministers, of different denominations, would come together and preach night and day, four or five days together ... In about thirty minutes the power of God fell on the congregation in such a manner as is seldom seen; the people fell in every direction, right and left, front and rear. It was supposed that not less than three hundred fell like dead men in mighty battle; and there was no need of calling mourners, for they were strewed all over the camp-ground ..." },
      { cite: "Peter Cartwright, Autobiography of Peter Cartwright, the Backwoods Preacher, ed. W. P. Strickland, New York: Carlton & Porter, 1857 (first published 1856), chapters 5 and 8, describing revivals in Kentucky and Ohio in the early 1800s. Internet Archive.", url: "https://archive.org/details/autobiographype00cartgoog", kind: "primary" },
    ] }],
  },
  "a4b-s11": {
    id: "a4b-s11", name: "Religion in the hands of the people", intro: "Use the passage below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "After the Revolution, American Christianity was remade from below. Upstart preachers, many with little schooling, challenged the authority of the college-trained clergy of the older churches. They told ordinary men and women that they could read the Bible for themselves, choose salvation by their own will, and judge preachers by their sincerity rather than their learning. They preached in plain speech, sang popular hymns, and organized new churches run by their members. In a nation that was opening politics to the common man, these movements made religion popular as well, and by the middle of the century the newest churches had become the largest in the country." },
      { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
    ] }],
  },
