  "a4b-s14": {
    id: "a4b-s14", name: "Prigg v. Pennsylvania, 1842", intro: "Use the excerpt below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "Historically, it is well known that the object of this clause was to secure to the citizens of the slave-holding States the complete right and title of ownership in their slaves, as property, in every State in the Union into which they might escape from the State where they were held in servitude. The full recognition of this right and title was indispensable to the security of this species of property in all the slave-holding States, and indeed was so vital to the preservation of their domestic interests and institutions that it cannot be doubted that it constituted a fundamental article without the adoption of which the Union could not have been formed. Its true design was to guard against the doctrines and principles prevalent in the non-slaveholding States, by preventing them from intermeddling with, or obstructing, or abolishing the rights of the owners of slaves." },
      { cite: "Justice Joseph Story, opinion of the Court, Prigg v. Pennsylvania, 41 U.S. 539 (1842), on the Constitution's fugitive slave clause.", url: "https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/41/539/", kind: "primary" },
    ] }],
  },
  "a4b-s15": {
    id: "a4b-s15", name: "Free Black communities in the North", intro: "Use the passage below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "Between 1790 and 1840 the free Black population of the United States grew more than sixfold, and its largest communities grew in Philadelphia, New York, Boston, and Baltimore. Shut out of white churches, schools, and many trades, free African Americans built institutions of their own: independent churches, schools, mutual aid and burial societies, and, by the late 1820s, their own newspapers and conventions. These communities lived under constant threat. Many states barred them from voting, white mobs attacked their neighborhoods, and kidnappers seized free people to sell into slavery. Yet the institutions they built gave Black Americans a public voice and became a base for the fight against slavery." },
      { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
    ] }],
  },
