  "a4b-s19": {
    id: "a4b-s19", name: "The market revolution and the regions", intro: "Use the passage below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "Between 1815 and 1848 the market revolution tied Americans together and pulled them apart at the same time. Canals, steamboats, and early railroads let farmers in the Old Northwest ship grain and pork east, and Northeastern cities and mills sent manufactured goods back. The South grew cotton for the mills of Britain and New England and bought much of its food and finished goods from other regions. Each region came to depend on the others, yet each also developed different interests. Manufacturers wanted protective tariffs that planters resented, and Westerners wanted federal roads that many Southerners opposed. At the same time, the growth of wage work and cities unsettled family and community life, and reformers set out to restore order." },
      { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
    ] }],
  },
  "a4b-s20": {
    id: "a4b-s20", name: "Two views of the reform impulse", intro: "Use the passages below to answer all parts of the question that follows.",
    sources: [
      { title: "Source 1", blocks: [
        { p: "The reform movements of the 1820s to the 1840s grew from the revivals of the Second Great Awakening. Revival preachers taught that people were free to choose salvation and that the converted should work to rid the world of sin. Many converts came to believe that society itself could be perfected. They formed societies to fight drinking, to end slavery, to care for the insane, and to spread schooling and Bibles. For these reformers, the campaign against social evils was an act of faith, and their sense of moral urgency explains why they demanded change that was immediate and complete rather than gradual." },
        { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
      ] },
      { title: "Source 2", blocks: [
        { p: "Antebellum reform is best understood as a response to the disorder created by the market revolution. As workshops gave way to factories and small towns grew into crowded cities, a new middle class of merchants, manufacturers, and professionals worried about drinking, crime, poverty, and the habits of wage workers and immigrants. Temperance pledges, public schools, prisons, and asylums promised to produce sober, punctual, and obedient citizens. Reform, in this view, was as much about controlling the lower classes and shaping a disciplined workforce as about saving souls, and the reformers' own economic interests shaped which evils they chose to attack." },
        { cite: "Practice passage written for this review, summarizing an interpretation held by many historians.", kind: "practice" },
      ] },
    ],
  },
  "a4b-s21": {
    id: "a4b-s21", name: "The Chickasaw refuse to exchange their lands, 1826", intro: "Use the excerpt below to answer all parts of the question that follows.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "We have laid before the council, this evening, the talk of our brothers the commissioners, and have come to the determination as follows:" },
      { p: "As we have no wish to exchange our lands here for any other, we think it unnecessary to run our father the President to any further expense; therefore, we have to refuse your propositions. If we were to accede to the offer made to us by our brothers the commissioners, in exploring that section of country west of the Mississippi river, it would incur great expense to our father the President for no effect, as we are entirely opposed to exchanging countries." },
      { cite: "Written answer of the Chickasaw council (signed by Levi Colbert and four other leaders) to the United States commissioners, Treaty Ground, Chickasaw Nation, November 1, 1826, in \"Refusal of the Chickasaws and Choctaws to Cede Their Lands in Mississippi: 1826,\" from Charles J. Kappler, ed., Indian Affairs: Laws and Treaties, vol. 2, Washington: Government Printing Office, 1904.", url: "https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/nt007.asp", kind: "primary" },
    ] }],
  },
